words={
 '慈善':('charity; benevolence','kango','noun, society',['寄付','援助','仁','奉仕','親切','寛大','救助'],'1',
 '慈善 is helping people out of compassion, especially by giving aid to people in need. Digital Daijisen defines it as 情けや哀れみをかけること (なさけやあわれみをかけること), “showing kindness and pity,” and adds 特に、恵まれない人々や被害にあった人々に経済的な援助をすること (とくに、めぐまれないひとびとやひがいにあったひとびとにけいざいてきなえんじょをすること), “in particular, giving financial aid to the underprivileged or to victims.” JMdict glosses it “charity; benevolence; philanthropy.”',
 'その歌手は毎年、慈善コンサートを開いている。 (そのかしゅはまいとし、じぜんコンサートをひらいている。) — That singer holds a charity concert every year.',
 '集まったお金は、すべて慈善団体に寄付された。 (あつまったおかねは、すべてじぜんだんたいにきふされた。) — All the money collected was donated to a charitable organization.',
 f'@慈 (じ) means compassion, and @善 (ぜん) here means goodness or a good deed; [Digital Daijisen, 善]({W("善")}) lists 慈善 under that sense, sense 1. 慈善 often comes directly before another noun, as in 慈善コンサート and 慈善団体 (じぜんだんたい), “a charitable organization.”',
 '&寄付 (きふ) is giving money or goods to a cause, and &援助 (えんじょ) is aid or support of any kind. &奉仕 (ほうし) is service or work for others or for a cause. 慈善 names charitable aid or activity itself, especially help for people in need.',
 'Related words are 慈悲 (じひ), “mercy; compassion,” 慈愛 (じあい), “affection (esp. parental),” 慈善家 (じぜんか), “a philanthropist,” and 慈善事業 (じぜんじぎょう), “philanthropic work.”'),
 '慕う':('long for; adore; look up to','wago','verb, feelings',['憧れる','恋しい','懐かしい','尊敬','敬う','愛する','崇拝','望む','感心'],'1–3',
 '慕う is to feel warm attachment to someone or something. Digital Daijisen gives three senses: to miss or long for, as in 遠い祖国を慕う (とおいそこくをしたう), “long for one’s distant homeland”; to follow someone one cannot bear to leave, as in 赤ん坊が母を慕って泣く (あかんぼうがははをしたってなく), “the baby cries after its mother”; and to be drawn to a superior’s character or learning, as in その徳を慕って人が集まる (そのとくをしたってひとがあつまる), “people gather, drawn by that person’s virtue.” JMdict lists the same three: “to yearn for; to adore,” “to follow (someone)” and “to idolize (for virtue, learning, status, etc.).”',
 '生徒たちはその先生を姉のように慕っていた。 (せいとたちはそのせんせいをあねのようにしたっていた。) — The students adored that teacher like an older sister.',
 '多くの若手が、その監督の人柄を慕って集まった。 (おおくのわかてが、そのかんとくのひとがらをしたってあつまった。) — Many younger people gathered around the coach, drawn by the coach’s character.',
 '@慕 alone writes した, and う is okurigana, the kana ending. 慕って (したって), in both examples, is the te-form. を marks the person or thing one is drawn to: 先生を慕う, 人柄を慕う.',
 '&憧れる (あこがれる) is to admire or yearn for something and marks what one admires with に, while 慕う takes を. &尊敬 (そんけい) is respect or esteem. &恋しい (こいしい) is an adjective for missing someone or something. 慕う is a verb of warm attachment to a person or place, and also of following someone one admires.',
 'Related words are 慕わしい (したわしい), “dear; beloved,” 思慕 (しぼ), “yearning; deep affection,” and 慕情 (ぼじょう), “longing; yearning.”'),
 '感慨':('deep emotion; strong feelings','kango','noun, feelings',['感動','感激','感情','しみじみ','実感','気持ち','胸','叙情','感じ','感ずる','感じる','しんみり','郷愁','懐かしい'],'1',
 '感慨 is a deep feeling that wells up in the heart. Digital Daijisen defines it as 心に深く感じて、しみじみとした気持ちになること (こころにふかくかんじて、しみじみとしたきもちになること), “feeling something deeply and being filled with a quiet, heartfelt mood,” and gives 感慨にひたる (かんがいにひたる), “be immersed in deep emotion.” JMdict glosses it “deep emotion; strong feelings.”',
 '三十年ぶりに母校を訪れ、感慨にひたった。 (さんじゅうねんぶりにぼこうをおとずれ、かんがいにひたった。) — Visiting my old school for the first time in thirty years, I was immersed in deep emotion.',
 '完成した橋を見て、工事に関わった人たちは感慨深い表情を浮かべた。 (かんせいしたはしをみて、こうじにかかわったひとたちはかんがいぶかいひょうじょうをうかべた。) — Looking at the finished bridge, the people who had worked on it looked deeply moved.',
 f'@感 (かん) is feeling, and @慨 (がい) means to be filled with stirring thoughts; [Digital Daijisen, 慨]({W("慨")}) lists 感慨 under 心を揺さぶる思いで一杯になる (こころをゆさぶるおもいでいっぱいになる), “to be filled with heart-stirring feelings.” 感慨深い (かんがいぶかい), in the second example, is an i-adjective; JMdict glosses it “deeply emotive; moving.”',
 '&感動 (かんどう) and &感激 (かんげき) are being moved or stirred by something. &しみじみ is an adverb for a feeling that sinks in deeply and quietly; Digital Daijisen uses it to define 感慨. JMdict lists 感慨 only as a noun, while 感動 and 感激 can also be する verbs; 感慨 is used in phrases such as 感慨にひたる and 感慨深い.',
 '感慨無量 (かんがいむりょう) is “being filled with deep emotion.” Do not confuse 感慨 with 灌漑 (かんがい), “irrigation,” which has the same reading.'),
 '憤慨':('indignation; resentment','kango','noun, suru-verb, feelings',['怒る','恨み','恨む','不満','むかむか','ぷりぷり','いらいら','嘆く'],'1',
 '憤慨 is getting very angry. Digital Daijisen defines it as ひどく腹を立てること (ひどくはらをたてること), “getting extremely angry,” and gives 身勝手なふるまいに憤慨する (みがってなふるまいにふんがいする), “be indignant at selfish behavior.” JMdict glosses it “indignation; resentment”; with する, it is a verb.',
 '住民たちは、市の一方的な決定に憤慨した。 (じゅうみんたちは、しのいっぽうてきなけっていにふんがいした。) — The residents were indignant at the city’s one-sided decision.',
 '約束を破られて、彼は「もう信用できない」と憤慨していた。 (やくそくをやぶられて、かれは「もうしんようできない」とふんがいしていた。) — After the promise to him was broken, he was indignant, saying, “I can’t trust them anymore.”',
 f'@憤 (ふん) means to be angry or indignant, and @慨 (がい) means to be filled with stirring feelings; [Digital Daijisen, 慨]({W("慨")}) lists 憤慨 under that sense. に marks the cause of the anger: 決定に憤慨した, 身勝手なふるまいに憤慨する.',
 '&怒る (おこる) is the everyday verb for getting angry. &恨む (うらむ) is to bear a grudge against someone. &不満 (ふまん) is dissatisfaction, which need not involve anger. &嘆く (なげく) is to lament or deplore; 憤慨 is anger rather than sorrow.',
 'Related words are 憤り (いきどおり), “resentment; indignation,” and 義憤 (ぎふん), “righteous indignation.”'),
 '慶弔':('congratulations and condolences','kango','noun, ceremony',['祝い','祝う','お祝い','弔問','葬式','おめでとう','香典','追悼','悔やむ','めでたい'],'1',
 '慶弔 covers happy and sad occasions together. Digital Daijisen defines it as 結婚・出産などの喜ぶべきことと、死などの悲しむべきこと (けっこん・しゅっさんなどのよろこぶべきことと、しなどのかなしむべきこと), “joyful events such as marriage or childbirth, and sad events such as death,” and also お祝い事と不幸 (おいわいごととふこう), “celebrations and bereavements.” JMdict glosses it “congratulations and condolences.”',
 '会社の規定で、慶弔の際には休暇が取れる。 (かいしゃのきていで、けいちょうのさいにはきゅうかがとれる。) — Under company rules, employees can take leave for celebrations and bereavements.',
 'この会社には、社員の結婚や家族の不幸に慶弔見舞金を出す制度がある。 (このかいしゃには、しゃいんのけっこんやかぞくのふこうにけいちょうみまいきんをだすせいどがある。) — This company has a scheme that pays an allowance when an employee marries or loses a family member.',
 f'@慶 (けい) here means a happy occasion, sense 2 in [Digital Daijisen, 慶]({W("慶")}), and @弔 (ちょう) means to mourn a death and offer condolences; [Digital Daijisen, 弔]({W("弔")}) lists 慶弔 under that sense. The word pairs the two opposite kinds of occasion.',
 '&お祝い (おいわい) is a celebration or a congratulatory gift, and &弔問 (ちょうもん) is a condolence call. 慶弔 names both kinds of occasion at once; JMdict lists 慶弔休暇 (けいちょうきゅうか), “special leave for births, marriages or deaths in the family,” and 慶弔見舞金 (けいちょうみまいきん), in the second example, “congratulatory and condolence allowances.”',
 f'The {JY}({W("慶弔")}) entry explains 慶弔 as 慶事 (けいじ), happy events, and 弔事 (ちょうじ), sad events: weddings and births are 慶事, and funerals are 弔事. 弔事 is a different word from 弔辞 (ちょうじ), “memorial address; eulogy,” which has the same reading.'),
 '憂慮':('anxiety; concern; fear','kango','noun, suru-verb, feelings',['心配','不安','悩む','関心','はらはら','恐れる','恐怖','関する','関わる','巡る','びくびく','かまう'],'1',
 '憂慮 is worrying about a situation. Digital Daijisen defines it as 心配すること。思いわずらうこと (しんぱいすること。おもいわずらうこと), “worrying; being troubled in one’s thoughts,” and gives 事態を憂慮する (じたいをゆうりょする), “be concerned about the situation.” JMdict glosses it “anxiety; concern; fear”; with する, it is a verb.',
 '専門家は、川の水質の悪化を憂慮している。 (せんもんかは、かわのすいしつのあっかをゆうりょしている。) — Experts are concerned about the worsening water quality of the river.',
 '紛争の長期化について、多くの国が深い憂慮を表明した。 (ふんそうのちょうきかについて、おおくのくにがふかいゆうりょをひょうめいした。) — Many countries expressed deep concern about the prolonged conflict.',
 f'@憂 (ゆう) means to worry, and @慮 (りょ) means to think things over; [Digital Daijisen, 慮]({W("慮")}) lists 憂慮 under that sense. を marks what one is concerned about: 悪化を憂慮している, 事態を憂慮する.',
 f'&心配 (しんぱい) is the everyday word for worry, and &不安 (ふあん) is a feeling of unease. The {JY}({W("憂慮")}) entry notes that 憂慮 usually means worrying with a strong sense of crisis, not merely being flustered with worry.',
 'Related words are 懸念 (けねん), “worry; concern,” and 一喜一憂 (いっきいちゆう), “swinging between joy and sorrow.”'),
 '遺憾':('regrettable; deplorable','kango','na-adjective, noun, evaluation',['残念','惜しい','悔しい','申し訳ない','気の毒','悔やむ','哀れ','嘆く','がっかり','失望','あいにく','不満'],'1',
 '遺憾 describes something that did not turn out as it should have and is regrettable. Digital Daijisen defines it as 期待したようにならず、心残りであること。残念に思うこと (きたいしたようにならず、こころのこりであること。ざんねんにおもうこと), “things not turning out as hoped, leaving dissatisfaction; feeling it is a pity,” and gives 遺憾の意を表する (いかんのいをひょうする), “express regret.” JMdict glosses it “regrettable; unsatisfactory; deplorable; lamentable.”',
 '政府は、隣国の一方的な発表に対し、遺憾の意を表明した。 (せいふは、りんごくのいっぽうてきなはっぴょうにたいし、いかんのいをひょうめいした。) — The government expressed regret over the neighboring country’s one-sided announcement.',
 'このような事故が起きたことは、誠に遺憾です。 (このようなじこがおきたことは、まことにいかんです。) — It is truly regrettable that such an accident has occurred.',
 f'@遺 (い) here means to remain behind, and @憾 (かん) means to feel strongly that something is unsatisfactory; [Digital Daijisen, 遺]({W("遺")}) lists 遺憾 under sense 1 of イ, 後に残る (あとにのこる), “remain behind.” JMdict classifies 遺憾 as both a na-adjective and a noun. Before a noun it takes な, as in 遺憾な点 (いかんなてん), “a regrettable aspect”; it is followed by の in the set phrase 遺憾の意 (いかんのい), “regret,” in the first example, and ends a sentence with です in the second.',
 f'&残念 (ざんねん) is the everyday word for regrettable or disappointing, and &悔しい (くやしい) is the frustration of losing or failing. The {JY}({W("遺憾")}) entry notes that 遺憾 is used mainly in formal settings, and that 遺憾の意を示す (いかんのいをしめす), “express regret,” is common in political and business statements.',
 'Two set phrases are 遺憾なく (いかんなく), “fully; amply,” as in 遺憾なく力を発揮する (いかんなくちからをはっきする), “show one’s ability to the full,” and 遺憾ながら (いかんながら), “I regret to say; unfortunately.”'),
 '遺産':('inheritance; legacy; heritage','kango','noun, money',['財産','相続','伝統'],'1',
 '遺産 is what someone leaves behind after death, and also what earlier generations have handed down. Digital Daijisen gives two senses: 死後に残した財産 (しごにのこしたざいさん), “property left after death,” and 前代の人が残した業績 (ぜんだいのひとがのこしたぎょうせき), “achievements left by people of an earlier age,” as in 文化遺産 (ぶんかいさん), “cultural heritage.” JMdict glosses it “inheritance; bequest; legacy; heritage.”',
 '祖父の遺産は、三人の子どもで分けることになった。 (そふのいさんは、さんにんのこどもでわけることになった。) — It was decided that Grandfather’s estate would be divided among his three children.',
 'この古い町並みは、地域の大切な遺産として守られている。 (このふるいまちなみは、ちいきのたいせつないさんとしてまもられている。) — These old streets are protected as an important heritage of the region.',
 f'@遺 (い) here means to leave behind after death, sense 2 of イ in [Digital Daijisen, 遺]({W("遺")}), and @産 (さん) means assets; [Digital Daijisen, 産]({W("産")}) lists 遺産 under sense 3, 生活に必要なもとで (せいかつにひつようなもとで), “the means needed for living.”',
 '&財産 (ざいさん) is property or wealth in general, including what a living person owns. &相続 (そうぞく) is the act of inheriting. &伝統 (でんとう) is a tradition handed down; 遺産 in its second sense is a legacy of achievements left by earlier generations.',
 '遺言 (ゆいごん), “a will; last words,” uses the other on reading of 遺; JMdict notes that it is read いごん in legal contexts. Related words are 遺族 (いぞく), “bereaved family,” and 世界遺産 (せかいいさん), “World Heritage (site).”'),
 '懇談':('friendly talk; informal meeting','kango','noun, suru-verb, communication',['相談','話し合う','話し合い','会話','打ち合わせ','会合','会議','集会','おしゃべり','しゃべる','話','話す','出会い','会','集まり','議論'],'1',
 '懇談 is talking together in a relaxed, friendly way. Digital Daijisen defines it as 打ち解けて親しく話し合うこと (うちとけてしたしくはなしあうこと), “opening up and talking together on friendly terms,” and gives 先生と父兄が懇談する (せんせいとふけいがこんだんする), “teachers and parents have a talk.” JMdict glosses it “friendly chat; relaxed conversation; informal meeting”; with する, it is a verb.',
 '学期末に、担任の先生と保護者の懇談がある。 (がっきまつに、たんにんのせんせいとほごしゃのこんだんがある。) — At the end of the term, there is a talk between the homeroom teacher and parents.',
 '社長は若手社員と昼食をとりながら懇談した。 (しゃちょうはわかてしゃいんとちゅうしょくをとりながらこんだんした。) — The company president chatted with young employees over lunch.',
 f'@懇 (こん) means warm sincerity, and @談 (だん) means to talk; [Digital Daijisen, 談]({W("談")}) lists 懇談 under that sense, sense 1. と marks the person one talks with: 若手社員と懇談した.',
 '&相談 (そうだん) is asking for advice or discussing a problem, and &会議 (かいぎ) is a meeting held for discussion, planning or decisions, large or small. &話し合い (はなしあい) is talking something over together. 懇談 stresses that the talk is friendly and relaxed.',
 'Related words are 懇談会 (こんだんかい), “an informal meeting; a get-together,” and 懇親会 (こんしんかい), “a social gathering; a get-together.”'),
 '懲りる':('learn a lesson; be put off','wago','verb, feelings',['反省','学ぶ','習う','教わる','うんざり','飽きる','課','授業','学習'],'1–2',
 '懲りる is to have such a bad experience that you resolve not to repeat it. Digital Daijisen defines it as 失敗してひどい目にあい、もうやるまいと思う (しっぱいしてひどいめにあい、もうやるまいとおもう), “to fail, suffer badly and resolve never to do it again,” and gives 二度の失敗ですっかり懲りた (にどのしっぱいですっかりこりた), “after failing twice, I had completely learned my lesson.” JMdict lists “to learn by experience; to learn one’s lesson” and “to be discouraged (by); to have enough (of).”',
 '一度ひどい船酔いをして懲りたので、もう船には乗らない。 (いちどひどいふなよいをしてこりたので、もうふねにはのらない。) — I got terribly seasick once and learned my lesson, so I don’t take boats anymore.',
 '彼は何度遅刻しても懲りない。 (かれはなんどちこくしてもこりない。) — No matter how many times he is late, he never learns.',
 '@懲 alone writes こ, and りる is okurigana. 懲りる is an ichidan verb: 懲りた (こりた) is the past and 懲りない (こりない) the negative. The experience that taught the lesson can be marked with に, as in the proverb 羹に懲りて膾を吹く below, or with で, as in Digital Daijisen’s 二度の失敗で.',
 '&反省 (はんせい) is reflecting on one’s own mistakes, and &学ぶ (まなぶ) is learning in general. &うんざり describes being fed up, and &飽きる (あきる) is growing tired of something. 懲りる is specifically being put off by a bad experience.',
 f'Related words are 懲らしめる (こらしめる), “to chastise; to punish,” 性懲りもなく (しょうこりもなく), “not having learned one’s lesson,” and こりごり (懲り懲り), “having had enough (of)”; JMdict marks こりごり as usually written in kana. JMdict glosses the proverb 羹に懲りて膾を吹く (あつものにこりてなますをふく) “a burnt child dreads the fire; once bitten twice shy.” [Digital Daijisen]({W("羹に懲りて膾を吹く")}) explains the image: burned by hot soup (羹, あつもの), one blows to cool even cold namasu (膾, なます), which JMdict describes as raw fish and vegetables seasoned in vinegar.'),
}
chars={
'慈':('慈 is compassion: tender, caring love. It appears in 慈善, in 慈悲 (じひ), “mercy; compassion,” and in 慈愛 (じあい), “affection (esp. parental).”',
 'The top part, 兹, is also the right side of @磁, “magnetism,” which is likewise read ジ; @心, “heart,” is at the bottom. Picture a heart full of tender care.',
 f'@磁 shares the top part and the reading ジ, but means magnetism or porcelain; @総 and @態 also end in 心 and mean whole and attitude. @愛, @恵, @恩, @仁, @徳 and @哀 relate to love, blessing, favor, benevolence, virtue or pity; @恋 and @慕 express longing; @惜 is to value something or find its loss a pity, and @慰 is to console. The [standard-use table, PDF p. 68]({JOYO}#page=68) lists ジ, with 慈愛, 慈善 and 慈悲, and いつくしむ, with 慈しむ.',
 {'いつく.しむ':('慈しむ (いつくしむ), to treat tenderly or love; 慈しみ (いつくしみ), affection or tenderness.','1315280 1315270')}),
'慕':('慕 means to long for, or be drawn with affection to, someone or something. It appears in 慕う, in 思慕 (しぼ), “yearning; deep affection,” and in 慕情 (ぼじょう), “longing.”',
 'The top part, 莫, is also the top of @募, “recruit,” @墓, “grave,” and @暮, “dusk,” which are all read ボ, like 慕. Below it is a four-stroke form of 心, “heart,” as at the bottom of @恭.',
 f'@募, @墓 and @暮 share the top part and the reading ボ, but mean recruit, grave and dusk. @恋, @憧 and @懐 express longing, yearning or fond attachment; @愛 is love; @敬, @尊, @崇 and @仰 express respect or reverence. The [standard-use table, PDF p. 140]({JOYO}#page=140) lists ボ, with 慕情, 敬慕 (けいぼ), “love and respect,” and 思慕, and したう, with 慕う and 慕わしい (したわしい), “dear; beloved.”',
 {'ボ':('慕情 (ぼじょう), longing or yearning; 思慕 (しぼ), yearning or deep affection.','1514920 1309600')}),
'慨':('慨 is a strong feeling that stirs the heart, whether deep emotion or indignant sorrow. It appears in 感慨, in 憤慨, “indignation,” and in 慨嘆 (がいたん), “lamentation; regret.”',
 '忄, the narrow form of 心 “heart,” is on the left, and @既, “already,” is on the right.',
 f'@概 has 木 in place of 忄 and is also read ガイ, but means outline or roughly; @既, the right-hand part on its own, is read キ and means already; @慣 also starts with 忄 and means accustomed. @嘆, @悲 and @哀 relate to lament or sorrow; @憤, @怒 and @憾 to indignation, anger or regret; @感, @激, @情 and @胸 to feeling, intensity, emotion or the heart. The [standard-use table, PDF p. 25]({JOYO}#page=25) gives 慨 only ガイ, with 慨嘆, 憤慨 and 感慨.',
 {'なげ.く':('source-only here: KANJIDIC2 records なげく, and Kanjipedia lists it outside standard use; JMdict’s なげく entry lists only the spellings 嘆く and 歎く, and “to lament” is written &嘆く (なげく). It is not used in practice questions.','1418090')}),
'慶':('慶 is joy at a happy occasion, and the happy occasion itself. It appears in 慶弔, in 慶事 (けいじ), “a happy event,” and in 慶祝 (けいしゅく), “celebration.”',
 '广 is at the top, like a roof; @鹿, “deer,” starts with the same top. Under it are @心, “heart,” and then 夂 at the bottom, the same order as in @愛, “love.”',
 f'@鹿 shares the top part and means deer; @愛 and @憂 also have 心 above 夂, but mean love and worry. @喜, @祝, @歓, @悦, @幸, @福, @吉, @愉, @楽 and @享 relate to joy, celebration, good fortune or enjoyment. The {JY}({W("慶")}) entry notes that 慶 is often used in expressions such as 謹んでお慶び申し上げます (つつしんでおよろこびもうしあげます), a formal congratulation. The [standard-use table, PDF p. 45]({JOYO}#page=45) gives 慶 only ケイ, with 慶弔, 慶祝 and 慶賀 (けいが), “congratulation.”',
 {'よろこ.び':('慶び: JMdict lists it as a rarely used kanji form of &喜び (よろこび), “joy,” and 慶ぶ as a rarely used form of &喜ぶ (よろこぶ), “to be delighted.” Kanjipedia lists よろこぶ outside standard use.','1606140 1218760')}),
'憂':('憂 is worry and grief: a heavy heart over what may happen or what has happened. It appears in 憂慮, in 憂鬱 (ゆううつ), “depression; melancholy,” and in 一喜一憂 (いっきいちゆう), “swinging between joy and sorrow.”',
 'Put 亻, the narrow form of 人 “person,” before 憂 and you get @優, “gentle; superior,” which is also read ユウ. In the middle of 憂 is @心, “heart,” and 夂 is at the bottom, as in @夏, “summer.”',
 f'@優 contains 憂 and is also read ユウ, but means gentle or superior; @夏 also ends in 夂, and @愛 also has 心 above 夂; they mean summer and love. @愁, @悩, @哀, @悲, @嘆, @悼 and @慨 relate to sorrow, worry, lament or mourning; @慮 is thinking something over, and @患 is to suffer or be ill. The [standard-use table, PDF p. 151]({JOYO}#page=151) lists ユウ, with 憂愁 (ゆうしゅう), “melancholy,” 憂慮 and 一喜一憂; うれえる, with 憂える and 憂え; うれい, with 憂い; and うい, with 憂い, 憂き目 and 物憂い. It notes that 憂き is the classical attributive form, and cross-references うれえる and うれい with 愁.',
 {'うれ.える':('憂える (うれえる), to worry about or lament; 憂え (うれえ), lamentation, grief or anxiety.','1588580 2149680'),
  'うれ.い':('憂い (うれい), a noun: sorrow or grief, and anxiety or worry. JMdict marks the sense “anxiety; worry” as especially 憂い and “sorrow; grief” as especially 愁い (うれい).','1540740'),
  'う.い':('憂い (うい), a literary adjective: unhappy or gloomy; 物憂い (ものうい), languid or listless. The same spelling 憂い is read うれい when it is the noun.','1540730 1502760'),
  'う.き':('憂き目 (うきめ), a bitter experience; the standard-use table notes that 憂き is the classical attributive form.','1588040')}),
'憤':('憤 is anger or indignation. It appears in 憤慨, in 憤り (いきどおり), “resentment; indignation,” and in 義憤 (ぎふん), “righteous indignation.”',
 '忄, the narrow form of 心 “heart,” is on the left; the right part is the same as in @噴, “erupt,” and @墳, “burial mound,” which are also read フン.',
 f'@噴 and @墳 share the right part and the reading フン, but mean erupt and burial mound; @慣 also starts with 忄 and means accustomed. @怒 is anger, @恨 resentment and @憎 hatred; @激 is intensity, as in 憤激 (ふんげき), “fury; outrage.” 発憤 (はっぷん), “being spurred on,” uses 憤 in the sense “rouse oneself”; JMdict lists it as a second spelling of 発奮. The [standard-use table, PDF p. 136]({JOYO}#page=136) lists フン, with 憤慨, 義憤 and 発憤, and いきどおる, with 憤る and 憤り.',
 {'いきどお.る':('憤る (いきどおる), to be angry or indignant; 憤り (いきどおり), resentment or indignation.','1504640 1857970')}),
'憾':('憾 is the feeling that something is unsatisfactory and regrettable. It appears in 遺憾, the only example the standard-use table gives for this kanji.',
 '忄, the narrow form of 心 “heart,” is on the left, and @感, “feeling,” is on the right; 感 is also read カン, like 憾. 感 already has 心 at the bottom, so 憾 has a heart in two places.',
 f'@感 forms the right part and is also read カン; @減 shares 咸, the top of 感, and means decrease; @憶 also starts with 忄 and means remember. @恨 and @悔 relate to resentment and regret, @惜 to finding something a pity, and @嘆 to lament. The [standard-use table, PDF p. 30]({JOYO}#page=30) gives 憾 only カン, with 遺憾.',
 {'うら.む':('憾む (うらむ), to regret; JMdict marks this entry as usually written in kana and also spells it 恨む. In &恨み (うらみ), JMdict marks the sense “matter for regret” as especially 憾み; the linked card teaches only the resentment sense. Kanjipedia lists うらむ outside standard use.','2833583 1289740')}),
'懇':('懇 is warm sincerity and friendliness. It appears in 懇談, in 懇切 (こんせつ), “kind and careful,” and in 懇親会 (こんしんかい), “a social gathering.”',
 'Above @心, “heart,” are 豸 on the left and 艮 on the right; 艮 is also the right side of @恨, “resent,” and @根, “root,” which are read コン, like 懇. @墾, “clear land,” has the same top over 土 and is also read コン.',
 f'@墾, @恨 and @根 share parts and the reading コン, but mean clear land for farming, resent and root. @親 (close), @厚 (cordial), @寧 (polite), @密 (close), @真 (true), @忠 (loyalty), @丹 (sincerity) and @允 (sincerity) overlap with warmth, closeness or sincerity. The [standard-use table, PDF p. 58]({JOYO}#page=58) lists コン, with 懇切 and 懇親会, and ねんごろ, with 懇ろだ.',
 {'ねんご.ろ':('懇ろ (ねんごろ), kind and courteous; JMdict marks it as usually written in kana.','1289820')}),
'懲':('懲 means to punish or chastise, and, as in 懲りる, to learn one’s lesson from a bad experience. It appears in 懲役 (ちょうえき), “imprisonment with hard labor,” and in 懲罰 (ちょうばつ), “discipline; punishment.”',
 '@徴, “sign; levy,” sits above @心, “heart”; 徴 is also read チョウ, like 懲.',
 f'@徴 forms the top and is also read チョウ, but means sign or levy; @微 and @徹 share 彳 and mean minute and penetrate. @罰, @刑, @罪 and @責 relate to punishment, crime or blame. @課 is titled “lesson,” as in a lesson in a textbook, and @習 and @学 relate to learning; they share only the English words “lesson” and “learn” with 懲’s “learn a lesson,” not its sense of being put off by a bad experience, and are kept out of its quiz choices so that the English does not mislead. The [standard-use table, PDF p. 111]({JOYO}#page=111) lists チョウ, with 懲罰, 懲戒 and 懲役; こりる, with 懲りる and 性懲りもなく (しょうこりもなく); こらす, with 懲らす; and こらしめる, with 懲らしめる.',
 {'チョウ':('懲罰 (ちょうばつ), discipline or punishment; 懲戒 (ちょうかい), discipline or reprimand.','1428210 1428200'),
  'こ.らす':('懲らす (こらす), to chastise or punish; JMdict marks it as dated. A different verb, &凝らす (こらす), means to concentrate effort.','1428180 1239010'),
  'こ.らしめる':('懲らしめる (こらしめる), to chastise, punish or discipline.','1428170')}),
'遺':('遺 means to leave something behind, especially after death, and also to be left behind. It appears in 遺産, in 遺憾, “regrettable,” where it means to remain behind, and in 遺言 (ゆいごん), “a will; last words.”',
 '辶, the “movement” part that wraps the lower left, runs under @貴, “precious.” @遣, “dispatch,” looks very similar, but a different part replaces 貝 at the bottom of 貴.',
 f'@遣 and @貴 look similar, but mean dispatch and precious and are read ケン and キ; @遅 also has 辶 and means late. @残, @留, @去 and @後 relate to remaining, staying, leaving or what comes after; @譲, @贈, @伝, @紹 and @継 to handing over, giving, passing on, introducing or succeeding; @失 and @忘 to losing and forgetting. The [standard-use table, PDF p. 13]({JOYO}#page=13) lists イ, with 遺棄 (いき), “abandonment,” 遺産 and 遺失 (いしつ), “loss,” and ユイ, with 遺言; it notes that 遺言 is also read イゴン.',
 {'ユイ':('遺言 (ゆいごん), a will or last words; JMdict notes that it is read いごん in legal contexts.','1159130'),
  'のこ.す':('遺す (のこす), to leave something to someone, especially after death; JMdict lists it as a separate entry, and the everyday &残す (のこす) also has the sense “to bequeath.” Kanjipedia lists のこす outside standard use.','2845927 1600260')}),
}
